  values (v_practice_tests, 'Test 2: Penicillin / Coral Reefs / Automation & Employment', 'test-2', 'A full IELTS Reading practice test (3 passages, 40 questions) from "IELTS Reading Band 5.5 to 8".', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"callout","attrs":{"variant":"note"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set a timer for 60 minutes (20 minutes per passage) before you start. Attempt the whole test without checking answers, then click Start Practice below to grade yourself and see the explanation for every question."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 1: The Discovery of Penicillin"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In September 1928, the Scottish bacteriologist Alexander Fleming returned from a summer holiday to his laboratory at St Mary’s Hospital in London to find something unusual. A petri dish in which he had been culturing Staphylococcus bacteria had become contaminated with a stray mould, and Fleming noticed that the area immediately surrounding the mould was completely free of bacterial growth. Curious, he identified the mould as belonging to the genus Penicillium, specifically Penicillium notatum, and correctly deduced that it was producing a substance capable of killing the surrounding bacteria — a substance he would later name penicillin."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fleming published his findings in 1929, but the discovery attracted remarkably little attention from the wider scientific community. He struggled to purify the compound into a stable form suitable for treating infections, and without a reliable method of production, he found it difficult to convince others of its therapeutic potential. For roughly a decade, research into penicillin as a practical medicine largely stalled."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It was not until the late 1930s that the discovery was revived, this time at Oxford University, by the Australian-born pathologist Howard Florey and the German-born biochemist Ernst Chain. Working together, the pair developed methods to extract and purify penicillin in sufficient quantity for proper testing. In 1941, they conducted the first human trial on a policeman suffering from a severe infection. His condition improved dramatically within days, but the limited supply of penicillin ran out before treatment could be completed, and he later died — a tragic outcome that nonetheless offered clear proof of the drug’s remarkable potential."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The outbreak of the Second World War created an urgent need for an effective treatment for infected wounds, and British and American governments, working alongside pharmaceutical companies, collaborated to scale up production using newly developed deep-fermentation techniques. By 1944, in time for the D-Day landings, penicillin was being produced in sufficient quantities to treat large numbers of wounded soldiers, saving countless lives that would previously have been lost to infection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In recognition of this transformative achievement, Fleming, Florey and Chain were jointly awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1945. Their work ushered in what is often called the antibiotic era, making it possible to treat bacterial infections that had previously been fatal and paving the way for complex surgery and treatments such as chemotherapy, which would otherwise carry an unacceptably high risk of infection."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Today, however, the very success of antibiotics like penicillin has given rise to a serious new concern. Decades of overuse and misuse have contributed to the emergence of antibiotic-resistant bacteria, and organisations such as the World Health Organization have warned of the possibility of a so-called post-antibiotic era if resistance continues to spread unchecked. Researchers around the world are now racing to discover entirely new classes of antibiotics as existing ones gradually lose their effectiveness."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 1–7"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Fleming discovered penicillin while deliberately searching for a new antibacterial substance."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Fleming identified the mould responsible for the effect as Penicillium notatum."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Scientific interest in Fleming’s discovery grew rapidly after his 1929 publication."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Florey and Chain were both British-born scientists."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The first human patient treated with penicillin ultimately died after supplies ran out."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Penicillin was available in large quantities in time to treat soldiers wounded in the Second World War."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Fleming personally attended the Nobel Prize ceremony in Stockholm."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 8–13"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Fleming made his discovery at St Mary’s Hospital in ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. The substance produced by the mould was later named ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Florey and Chain developed methods to ........... and purify penicillin in sufficient quantity for testing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. The first trial involved a patient who was a ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. By ........... , penicillin was available in significant quantities to treat wounded soldiers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"13. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. In 1945, Fleming, Florey and Chain were jointly awarded the ........... ."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2: Coral Reefs Under Threat"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Although coral reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, they are estimated to support roughly a quarter of all known marine species, earning them the popular nickname “rainforests of the sea”. A reef is built not by a single organism but by vast colonies of tiny animals called polyps, which slowly secrete calcium carbonate skeletons that, over hundreds or even thousands of years, accumulate into the elaborate structures divers recognise today."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The vivid colours associated with healthy reefs come from a remarkable partnership between coral polyps and microscopic algae called zooxanthellae, which live within the polyps’ tissues. Through photosynthesis, these algae generate food and energy that the coral relies on to survive, while in return the coral provides the algae with shelter and the compounds they need to photosynthesise effectively. Neither partner thrives without the other."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"This delicate partnership is highly sensitive to temperature. When surrounding water becomes even slightly warmer than usual for a sustained period, stressed coral polyps expel their zooxanthellae, causing the coral to turn a stark white in a process known as bleaching. Bleached coral is not immediately dead, but it is severely weakened and considerably more vulnerable to disease and starvation. As ocean temperatures continue to rise as a result of climate change, bleaching events have become alarmingly more frequent."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"A second, less visible threat comes from ocean acidification. Oceans absorb a significant proportion of the carbon dioxide released into the atmosphere, and this process forms carbonic acid, gradually lowering the water’s pH. In more acidic conditions, coral finds it considerably harder to build and maintain its calcium carbonate skeleton, which slows growth and weakens existing reef structures."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Beyond these global pressures, reefs also face a range of more localised threats. Agricultural runoff introduces excess nutrients and pollutants into coastal waters, overfishing disrupts the delicate balance of reef ecosystems, and unsustainable tourism practices, including careless anchoring, can cause direct physical damage to fragile coral structures. Coastal development can also increase sedimentation, clouding the water and blocking the sunlight that polyps and their algal partners depend on."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"In response, marine protected areas have been established in numerous regions, restricting fishing and anchoring in particularly vulnerable zones. Coral restoration projects, in which fragments are grown in nurseries before being transplanted onto damaged reefs, are also becoming increasingly common, and some researchers are experimenting with breeding more heat-resistant coral strains. Scientists are quick to caution, however, that local conservation efforts alone cannot fully offset the impact of global warming, meaning that meaningful reef protection ultimately depends on both local action and a genuine reduction in global emissions."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Question 14-19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. (Questions 14–19)"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"i. A vital but fragile partnership between two organisms"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ii. Local human activities compounding a global problem"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iii. Why warmer water can turn coral white"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iv. Efforts to protect and restore damaged reefs"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"v. An overlooked chemical threat from the atmosphere"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"vi. A structure built by tiny creatures over centuries"}]}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 20–26"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a description of how coral obtains most of its energy"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"21. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a reference to a change in the ocean’s chemical composition"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"22. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. an example of damage caused directly by visitors to reef areas"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"23. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a mention of scientists attempting to develop more resilient coral"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"24. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What term describes the tiny algae that live inside coral polyps?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"25. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What is the process called when coral expels its algae and turns white?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"26. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What do agricultural runoff and overfishing represent, according to the passage?"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 3: The Future of Work: Automation and Employment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The question of whether machines will ultimately replace human workers is far from new, but it has taken on fresh urgency amid recent advances in artificial intelligence and robotics. Historically, waves of technological change — from the mechanisation of agriculture to the introduction of assembly-line robots — have eliminated certain categories of work while simultaneously creating others. The central question today is whether the current wave is fundamentally different, given that it increasingly affects cognitive tasks, not merely manual ones."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Optimists point to a reassuring historical pattern: despite repeated predictions of mass unemployment following previous waves of automation, structural unemployment has not risen over the long term, and new industries and job categories have consistently emerged to absorb displaced workers. Many economists regard this pattern as grounds for cautious confidence about the future."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There is, however, reason for caution. The present wave of change may genuinely be different, both because it is unfolding considerably faster than previous transitions, leaving workers and institutions with less time to adapt, and because the range of tasks now capable of being automated spans both manual and white-collar professions simultaneously, unlike earlier waves that were largely confined to manual labour."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Certain sectors appear particularly exposed. Roles involving routine, predictable tasks — including basic data entry, simple bookkeeping, some forms of legal document review, and increasingly, transportation, through the gradual development of self-driving vehicles — are considered especially vulnerable. By contrast, sectors that depend heavily on complex interpersonal skills, creativity or unpredictable physical dexterity, such as healthcare, skilled trades and the creative industries, appear comparatively insulated from automation, at least for the foreseeable future."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In my view, the policy response to automation matters considerably more than the underlying technology itself. Sensible measures would include sustained investment in retraining programmes, the development of portable benefits that are not tied to a single employer, and a broader rethinking of education systems to emphasise adaptability over narrow technical training. Societies that prepare proactively for this transition are likely to fare far better than those that wait passively for disruption to occur before responding."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 27–31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"27. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, what makes the current wave of automation potentially different from earlier ones?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. It is affecting rural areas more than cities"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. It affects a broader range of tasks, including cognitive ones, more quickly"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. It has already caused unemployment to rise sharply"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. It has been almost entirely reversed by new legislation"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"28. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What do the “optimists” mentioned in the passage point to as reassurance?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Government guarantees of lifetime employment"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. The historical pattern of new jobs emerging after previous automation waves"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Evidence that automation always slows down after a decade"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. A complete absence of job losses in any previous period"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"29. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, which sectors are considered comparatively insulated from automation for now?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Data entry and basic bookkeeping"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. Transportation and logistics"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Healthcare, skilled trades and creative industries"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Legal document review"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What does the author suggest matters more than the technology itself?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. The speed of internet connectivity"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. The policy response to automation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. The number of robots produced annually"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Consumer demand for automated products"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"31. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which of the following does the author recommend?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Banning automation in white-collar professions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. Reducing investment in education"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Retraining programmes and portable benefits"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Waiting until disruption occurs before acting"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 32–36"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"32. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (32). Summary: Historically, technological change has both eliminated and created (32)........... , a pattern that optimists find reassuring. However, the author cautions that the present wave of automation may differ because it is faster and affects both manual and (33)........... tasks. Roles involving routine, predictable work are considered most at (34)........... , while those requiring interpersonal skills or creativity appear relatively protected. The author argues that the (35)........... response to automation is more important than the technology itself, recommending retraining programmes and (36)........... not tied to a single employer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"33. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (33). Summary: Historically, technological change has both eliminated and created (32)........... , a pattern that optimists find reassuring. However, the author cautions that the present wave of automation may differ because it is faster and affects both manual and (33)........... tasks. Roles involving routine, predictable work are considered most at (34)........... , while those requiring interpersonal skills or creativity appear relatively protected. The author argues that the (35)........... response to automation is more important than the technology itself, recommending retraining programmes and (36)........... not tied to a single employer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"34. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (34). Summary: Historically, technological change has both eliminated and created (32)........... , a pattern that optimists find reassuring. However, the author cautions that the present wave of automation may differ because it is faster and affects both manual and (33)........... tasks. Roles involving routine, predictable work are considered most at (34)........... , while those requiring interpersonal skills or creativity appear relatively protected. The author argues that the (35)........... response to automation is more important than the technology itself, recommending retraining programmes and (36)........... not tied to a single employer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"35. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (35). Summary: Historically, technological change has both eliminated and created (32)........... , a pattern that optimists find reassuring. However, the author cautions that the present wave of automation may differ because it is faster and affects both manual and (33)........... tasks. Roles involving routine, predictable work are considered most at (34)........... , while those requiring interpersonal skills or creativity appear relatively protected. The author argues that the (35)........... response to automation is more important than the technology itself, recommending retraining programmes and (36)........... not tied to a single employer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"36. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (36). Summary: Historically, technological change has both eliminated and created (32)........... , a pattern that optimists find reassuring. However, the author cautions that the present wave of automation may differ because it is faster and affects both manual and (33)........... tasks. Roles involving routine, predictable work are considered most at (34)........... , while those requiring interpersonal skills or creativity appear relatively protected. The author argues that the (35)........... response to automation is more important than the technology itself, recommending retraining programmes and (36)........... not tied to a single employer."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 37–40"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"37. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author believes the current wave of automation is definitely different from all previous waves."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"38. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author suggests that jobs requiring creativity are currently less vulnerable to automation."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"39. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author has personally advised governments on automation policy."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author argues that societies which prepare in advance will fare better than those that do not."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]}]}'::jsonb, 'published', 'advanced', 60, now())
  returning id into v_lesson;

  values (v_lesson, 'Practice: Test 2: Penicillin / Coral Reefs / Automation & Employment', 'Grade your answers and see the explanation for every question.', 'advanced', 60, 40, true)
  returning id into v_qset;




































  -- Test 3
  values (v_practice_tests, 'Test 3: The Silk Road / Renewable Energy / Language Acquisition', 'test-3', 'A full IELTS Reading practice test (3 passages, 40 questions) from "IELTS Reading Band 5.5 to 8".', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"callout","attrs":{"variant":"note"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set a timer for 60 minutes (20 minutes per passage) before you start. Attempt the whole test without checking answers, then click Start Practice below to grade yourself and see the explanation for every question."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 1: The Silk Road: Trade Across Continents"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contrary to what its name suggests, the Silk Road was never a single continuous road. Rather, it was a vast network of overland trade routes connecting China with Central Asia, the Middle East and, eventually, Europe, first established during the Han Dynasty in roughly the second century BCE. The evocative name itself is considerably more recent than the routes it describes: it was coined only in the nineteenth century by a German geographer named Richthofen, long after the network’s peak."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Silk was the commodity that gave the network its name, and for centuries the secret of its manufacture was closely guarded within China. Yet silk was far from the only good in transit: traders also carried spices, precious stones, glassware and, later, paper and gunpowder. Goods rarely travelled the entire route with a single merchant; instead, they typically passed through the hands of numerous middlemen, changing caravans at bustling oasis towns along the way."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Silk Road’s significance extended well beyond commerce. It served as a conduit for the exchange of religion — Buddhism, for instance, spread from India into China largely along these trade routes — as well as technology, including the westward spread of papermaking, and ideas more broadly. Cities such as Samarkand and Kashgar flourished as a result, becoming prosperous, multicultural hubs where merchants, scholars and travellers from vastly different backgrounds regularly crossed paths."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alongside the overland network, a maritime Silk Route also existed, connecting Chinese ports to Southeast Asia, India and eventually East Africa and the Arabian Peninsula. This sea-based trade became especially significant during the Tang and Song dynasties, as advances in shipbuilding and navigation made longer sea voyages considerably more practical and profitable."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The overland routes gradually declined in importance after the fifteenth century, partly as a result of European voyages of exploration, which sought a direct sea route to Asia that would bypass Central Asian middlemen entirely. Ottoman control over several key land routes further increased costs and risk for overland traders, accelerating the shift towards maritime trade."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The term has, however, experienced something of a revival in recent years. China’s Belt and Road Initiative, launched in 2013, explicitly invokes the historic Silk Road as inspiration for a vast programme of infrastructure investment stretching across Asia, Africa and Europe."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 1–7"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The Silk Road consisted of a single continuous road connecting China to Europe."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The term “Silk Road” was already in use during the Han Dynasty."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Most goods along the Silk Road were transported by camel rather than any other means."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Buddhism spread into China partly as a result of Silk Road trade routes."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The maritime Silk Route became especially significant during the Tang and Song dynasties."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"European voyages of exploration contributed to the decline of the overland Silk Road."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"China’s Belt and Road Initiative was launched in the nineteenth century."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 8–13"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. The term “Silk Road” was coined by a German geographer named ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Besides silk, traders also carried spices, precious stones and ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Cities such as Samarkand and Kashgar became prosperous, multicultural ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. The maritime route connected Chinese ports to Southeast Asia, India and eventually ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. ........... control of key land routes increased costs and risk for traders."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"13. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. China’s Belt and Road Initiative was launched in ........... ."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2: Renewable Energy: Solar and Wind Power"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Driven by mounting concern over climate change and steadily falling technology costs, the world has undergone a marked shift towards renewable sources of electricity over the past two decades. Solar and wind power, once dismissed as niche and prohibitively expensive, are now among the fastest-growing sources of electricity generation globally, and in many markets they have become genuinely cost-competitive with fossil fuels."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Solar electricity is generated using photovoltaic, or PV, cells, which convert sunlight directly into electricity through semiconductor materials, typically silicon. The cost of PV panels has fallen by more than eighty per cent over the past decade alone, driven by improvements in manufacturing scale and underlying technology. While solar remains especially effective in sunny regions, efficiency gains mean it is now a viable option even in comparatively cloudy northern climates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Wind power, meanwhile, relies on turbines that convert the kinetic energy of moving air into electricity through rotating blades connected to a generator. Onshore wind is a mature and relatively inexpensive technology, while offshore wind, though considerably more expensive to install, benefits from stronger and more consistent winds at sea. Offshore wind capacity has grown particularly rapidly in parts of Europe in recent years."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Both solar and wind share an inherent limitation: unlike traditional fossil fuel plants, which can generate electricity on demand, they are inherently variable, since the sun does not always shine and the wind does not always blow. This intermittency is increasingly addressed through a combination of battery storage, interconnection across regional electricity grids, and the retention of some flexible backup generation for periods of low output."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The renewable sector now employs millions of people worldwide, and falling costs mean that in many markets, building new solar or wind capacity is now cheaper than constructing a new fossil fuel plant. This transition is not without complications, however, as it also requires the retraining of workers whose livelihoods have traditionally depended on declining fossil fuel industries."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Despite falling generation costs, several obstacles remain. Lengthy permitting delays, insufficient transmission infrastructure to carry electricity from often-remote generation sites to distant urban demand centres, and the continued need for investment in storage technology all pose significant challenges. Increasingly, researchers and policymakers agree that infrastructure investment is now just as important as improvements in generation technology itself."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Question 14-19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. (Questions 14–19)"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"i. Converting sunlight directly into usable power"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ii. The problem of inconsistent supply and how it is managed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iii. Harnessing the power of moving air"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iv. Remaining obstacles beyond the cost of generation"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"v. A global shift towards cheaper clean energy"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"vi. The economic benefits and workforce implications"}]}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 20–26"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a comparison between two different locations for installing the same technology"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"21. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a reference to the percentage by which a technology’s cost has fallen"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"22. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a mention of methods used to cope with unpredictable energy supply"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"23. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a reference to infrastructure needed to move electricity from where it is produced to where it is needed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"24. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What type of cells convert sunlight directly into electricity?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"25. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What material is typically used to make these cells?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"26. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What must workers from declining industries undergo as part of the renewable transition?"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 3: Child Development and Language Acquisition"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Human infants acquire language with astonishing speed, progressing from producing no words at all to forming complex, grammatically structured sentences within just a few years, and typically without any formal instruction. This process remains one of the most intensively studied phenomena in cognitive science, and a central debate concerns just how much of this remarkable capacity is innate, as opposed to learned entirely from the surrounding environment."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One influential position, closely associated with the linguist Noam Chomsky, proposes that humans are born with an innate language faculty, sometimes referred to as universal grammar: a set of grammatical principles common to all human languages that allows a child to acquire whichever language they happen to be exposed to remarkably quickly, despite input that is often limited and imperfect. This has become known as the poverty of stimulus argument."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An alternative, usage-based view argues that language acquisition can instead be explained through general cognitive and learning mechanisms — pattern recognition, statistical learning and imitation — without needing to assume any dedicated, language-specific innate module. Proponents of this view point to substantial evidence that infants are highly sensitive to statistical regularities in the speech they hear from a very early age."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In my view, this debate is often presented as more binary than the evidence genuinely supports. Converging research increasingly suggests that language acquisition likely depends on a combination of domain-general learning mechanisms and certain innate biases or predispositions specific to language, rather than either a purely innate grammar or purely general-purpose learning acting alone. Most contemporary researchers now occupy something of a middle ground between the two original positions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A related but distinct idea is the critical period hypothesis, which proposes that there exists an optimal window, roughly before the onset of puberty, during which language acquisition occurs most readily. Evidence drawn from rare cases of severely language-deprived children, as well as studies of adult second-language learners, suggests that achieving native-like fluency becomes considerably harder, though not strictly impossible, once this window has closed. The precise boundaries of this critical period, however, remain a subject of ongoing debate among researchers."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 27–31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"27. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, one of the central debates in language acquisition concerns"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. whether children can learn more than one language"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. how much of language capacity is innate versus learned"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. the age at which children begin speaking"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. whether animals can acquire human language"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"28. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What is the “poverty of stimulus” argument associated with?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. children from economically disadvantaged backgrounds"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. the idea that children’s linguistic input is too limited to fully explain their rapid acquisition"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. a lack of research funding for language studies"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. children who are raised without any exposure to language"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"29. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, the usage-based view proposes that language is acquired through"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. an innate grammar module unique to humans"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. formal instruction from caregivers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. general learning mechanisms such as pattern recognition"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. exposure to written text only"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What is the author’s own position on the nativist versus usage-based debate?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. The nativist view is entirely correct"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. The usage-based view is entirely correct"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Both views are equally irrelevant to modern research"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. A combination of innate biases and general learning mechanisms is likely involved"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"31. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, what does research on the critical period hypothesis suggest?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Language acquisition is impossible after puberty"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. Native-like fluency becomes harder, though not impossible, to achieve later in life"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. There is no difference in language learning ability at any age"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Only severely deprived children are affected by timing"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 32–36"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"32. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (32). Summary: Human infants acquire language with remarkable speed and without formal (32)........... . One influential view, associated with Noam Chomsky, proposes that humans possess an innate (33)........... that enables rapid acquisition despite limited input. An alternative, usage-based view instead emphasises general (34)........... mechanisms such as pattern recognition. The author suggests that most researchers now occupy a (35)........... position between these two extremes. Separately, the critical period hypothesis suggests there is an optimal window, roughly before (36)........... , during which language is acquired most easily."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"33. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (33). Summary: Human infants acquire language with remarkable speed and without formal (32)........... . One influential view, associated with Noam Chomsky, proposes that humans possess an innate (33)........... that enables rapid acquisition despite limited input. An alternative, usage-based view instead emphasises general (34)........... mechanisms such as pattern recognition. The author suggests that most researchers now occupy a (35)........... position between these two extremes. Separately, the critical period hypothesis suggests there is an optimal window, roughly before (36)........... , during which language is acquired most easily."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"34. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (34). Summary: Human infants acquire language with remarkable speed and without formal (32)........... . One influential view, associated with Noam Chomsky, proposes that humans possess an innate (33)........... that enables rapid acquisition despite limited input. An alternative, usage-based view instead emphasises general (34)........... mechanisms such as pattern recognition. The author suggests that most researchers now occupy a (35)........... position between these two extremes. Separately, the critical period hypothesis suggests there is an optimal window, roughly before (36)........... , during which language is acquired most easily."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"35. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (35). Summary: Human infants acquire language with remarkable speed and without formal (32)........... . One influential view, associated with Noam Chomsky, proposes that humans possess an innate (33)........... that enables rapid acquisition despite limited input. An alternative, usage-based view instead emphasises general (34)........... mechanisms such as pattern recognition. The author suggests that most researchers now occupy a (35)........... position between these two extremes. Separately, the critical period hypothesis suggests there is an optimal window, roughly before (36)........... , during which language is acquired most easily."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"36. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer. Blank (36). Summary: Human infants acquire language with remarkable speed and without formal (32)........... . One influential view, associated with Noam Chomsky, proposes that humans possess an innate (33)........... that enables rapid acquisition despite limited input. An alternative, usage-based view instead emphasises general (34)........... mechanisms such as pattern recognition. The author suggests that most researchers now occupy a (35)........... position between these two extremes. Separately, the critical period hypothesis suggests there is an optimal window, roughly before (36)........... , during which language is acquired most easily."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 37–40"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"37. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author believes the nativist and usage-based views are entirely incompatible with one another."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"38. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author suggests that most contemporary researchers hold a moderate position combining both views."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"39. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author has conducted original research specifically on the critical period hypothesis."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author argues that the exact boundaries of the critical period are firmly established and undisputed."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]}]}'::jsonb, 'published', 'advanced', 60, now())
  returning id into v_lesson;

  values (v_lesson, 'Practice: Test 3: The Silk Road / Renewable Energy / Language Acquisition', 'Grade your answers and see the explanation for every question.', 'advanced', 60, 40, true)
  returning id into v_qset;




































  -- Test 4
  values (v_practice_tests, 'Test 4: Bird Migration / Urbanisation & Health / Decision-Making', 'test-4', 'A full IELTS Reading practice test (3 passages, 40 questions) from "IELTS Reading Band 5.5 to 8".', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"callout","attrs":{"variant":"note"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set a timer for 60 minutes (20 minutes per passage) before you start. Attempt the whole test without checking answers, then click Start Practice below to grade yourself and see the explanation for every question."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 1: The Mystery of Bird Migration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every year, billions of birds undertake extraordinary journeys spanning thousands of kilometres between their breeding grounds and their non-breeding grounds. The Arctic tern holds the remarkable record for the longest known migration of any animal species, travelling from its Arctic breeding grounds to Antarctic waters and back again annually — a round trip of some seventy thousand kilometres."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Exactly how birds navigate across such vast distances puzzled scientists for centuries. Research now shows that birds rely on multiple overlapping cues: the position of the sun during the day, patterns of stars at night, and, most remarkably, an ability to detect the Earth’s magnetic field, a sense known as magnetoreception, thought to depend on specialised proteins located in the eye that are sensitive to magnetic fields."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The timing of migration is triggered primarily by changes in day length, or photoperiod, which in turn affects hormone levels. Some species also respond to temperature changes and food availability, though photoperiod is generally considered the most reliable and consistent cue, since, unlike the weather, it does not vary unpredictably from one year to the next."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Migration is an extraordinarily risky undertaking. Birds cross oceans, deserts and mountain ranges with little or no opportunity to rest or refuel along the way. It is estimated that a substantial proportion of migratory birds, particularly first-year juveniles undertaking their first migration without experienced adults to guide them, do not survive the journey."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Human activity has introduced further threats. Light pollution from cities disorients night-migrating birds, frequently causing fatal collisions with buildings, while habitat loss at stopover sites, where birds rest and refuel midway through their journey, can be every bit as damaging as the loss of breeding habitat itself. A bird unable to find sufficient food at a critical stopover site may simply lack the energy reserves needed to complete its journey."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In response, international agreements now protect networks of critical stopover sites across multiple countries, in recognition of the fact that migratory birds pay no attention to national borders. “Lights out” campaigns, which encourage cities to dim non-essential lighting during peak migration seasons, have shown measurable reductions in bird collision deaths wherever they have been adopted."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 1–7"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The Arctic tern undertakes the longest known migration of any animal species."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Scientists fully understood how birds navigate several centuries ago."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Scientists have identified the exact evolutionary origin of magnetoreception in birds."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Day length is described as a more reliable migration trigger than weather-related factors."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"All juvenile birds are guided by experienced adults during their first migration."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Loss of habitat at stopover sites can be as damaging as loss of breeding habitat."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"International conservation agreements have completely eliminated the risk of building collisions."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 8–13"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. The Arctic tern travels between its Arctic breeding grounds and ........... waters."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Birds’ ability to detect the Earth’s magnetic field is known as ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Migration timing is triggered primarily by changes in ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Habitat loss at ........... sites can be as damaging as loss of breeding habitat."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. ........... from cities disorients night-migrating birds."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"13. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. “Lights out” campaigns have shown measurable reductions in bird ........... deaths."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2: Urbanisation and Public Health"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"More than half of the world’s population now lives in urban areas, and this proportion is expected to keep rising in the decades ahead. Cities have historically offered better access to formal healthcare and sanitation infrastructure than rural areas, contributing to measurable improvements in public health over time. At the same time, urbanisation introduces a distinct set of new health challenges of its own."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Vehicle emissions and concentrated industrial activity mean that air pollution levels in many major cities frequently exceed World Health Organization guidelines. This is linked to a range of respiratory and cardiovascular problems, and poorer neighbourhoods, which are often located closer to industrial zones and busy roads, tend to be disproportionately affected."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Urban design has historically prioritised cars over pedestrians and cyclists in many cities, contributing to increasingly sedentary lifestyles. Easy access to processed, calorie-dense food, combined with reduced everyday physical activity, has been linked to rising rates of obesity among urban populations worldwide."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Higher population density, noise and the generally faster pace of urban life have been associated with elevated rates of certain mental health conditions, including anxiety and mood disorders. However, cities also offer greater access to specialised mental health services and more diverse social support networks, which complicates any simple conclusion about whether city living is, on balance, harmful to mental wellbeing."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"High population density in cities facilitates the rapid transmission of infectious disease, a fact demonstrated clearly during the recent global pandemic. Overcrowded informal settlements with inadequate sanitation infrastructure are particularly vulnerable to outbreaks of water-borne and other communicable diseases."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"There is growing recognition that thoughtful urban design can directly promote better health outcomes. Green spaces have been shown to reduce stress and encourage physical activity, investment in pedestrian and cycling infrastructure can reduce both pollution and sedentary behaviour, and mixed-use zoning, which places housing closer to everyday amenities, reduces reliance on cars. The so-called “healthy city” movement now influences planning policy in a growing number of municipalities worldwide."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Question 14-19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. (Questions 14–19)"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"i. A growing trend that brings both benefits and challenges"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ii. Pollution levels and who is most affected"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iii. How urban living patterns contribute to rising obesity"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iv. The mental health effects of city living are not straightforward"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"v. Cities and the rapid spread of infectious disease"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"vi. Designing urban spaces with health in mind"}]}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 20–26"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a reference to World Health Organization guidelines being exceeded"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"21. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a mention of a specific recent global health event"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"22. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. an explanation of why certain mental health effects are difficult to generalise"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"23. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. examples of specific design features that can improve urban health"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"24. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What proportion of the world’s population now lives in urban areas?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"25. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What type of settlements are described as particularly vulnerable to disease outbreaks?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"26. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What movement now influences planning policy in a growing number of municipalities?"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 3: The Psychology of Decision-Making"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Traditional economic models long assumed that human beings make decisions through careful, rational cost-benefit analysis, weighing all available information in order to select the objectively best option available — an approach often referred to as the rational actor model. Decades of research in behavioural psychology and economics, however, have revealed that actual human decision-making deviates from this ideal in predictable, systematic ways."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These systematic patterns of deviation are known as cognitive biases. Examples include confirmation bias, the tendency to favour information that confirms existing beliefs while discounting contradictory evidence; anchoring bias, an excessive reliance on the first piece of information encountered when making subsequent judgements; and loss aversion, the tendency to feel losses more intensely than equivalent gains, first documented in detail by the psychologists Daniel Kahneman and Amos Tversky."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An influential framework known as dual-process theory proposes two distinct systems of thought. System 1 is fast, automatic and intuitive, operating largely below the level of conscious awareness, while System 2 is slower, more deliberate and considerably more effortful, and is typically engaged only for complex reasoning tasks. Most everyday decisions rely heavily on System 1, which is generally efficient but correspondingly more susceptible to the biases described above."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Critics sometimes portray these biases as evidence that human decision-making is fundamentally flawed or irrational. This framing, while understandable, somewhat misses the point. Many of these apparent biases likely represent efficient heuristics that served our ancestors well in the environments in which human cognition originally evolved, even if they occasionally produce suboptimal outcomes in the more complex, information-rich environment of modern life."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This understanding of cognitive bias has, in turn, influenced so-called nudge theory, in which choice architecture is subtly adjusted to encourage better decisions without restricting anyone’s freedom of choice — automatic enrolment in a retirement savings scheme, which employees remain free to opt out of, is a frequently cited example. The approach has since been adopted by numerous governments and organisations worldwide, though it remains a subject of ongoing ethical debate concerning the boundary between helpful nudging and outright manipulation."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 27–31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"27. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, the “rational actor” model assumes that people"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. always make emotional decisions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. carefully weigh all available information to choose the best option"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. never consider costs or benefits"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. rely entirely on intuition"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"28. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What is loss aversion, as described in the passage?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. A tendency to avoid making any decisions at all"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. The tendency to feel losses more intensely than equivalent gains"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. A preference for taking financial risks"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. The inability to recognise that a loss has occurred"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"29. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to dual-process theory, which system is engaged for complex reasoning?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. System 1"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. System 2"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Both systems equally at all times"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Neither system, according to the theory"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What is the author’s own view of cognitive biases?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. They prove human decision-making is fundamentally broken"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. They likely represent heuristics that were useful in ancestral environments"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. They have no relevance to modern decision-making"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. They only affect a small minority of people"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"31. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, what is “nudge” theory concerned with?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Banning poor choices entirely"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. Subtly adjusting choice architecture to encourage better decisions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Forcing people to make particular decisions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Removing all elements of choice from decision-making"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 32–36"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"32. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (32). Summary: Traditional economic models assumed that people make decisions through careful (32)........... analysis. However, research has shown those decisions often deviate from this ideal due to (33)........... such as confirmation bias and loss aversion. Dual-process theory proposes two systems of thought: a fast, automatic (34)........... and a slower, more deliberate one used for complex reasoning. The author argues that many biases likely served as useful (35)........... in ancestral environments. This understanding has influenced (36)........... theory, which subtly adjusts choice architecture to encourage better decisions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"33. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (33). Summary: Traditional economic models assumed that people make decisions through careful (32)........... analysis. However, research has shown those decisions often deviate from this ideal due to (33)........... such as confirmation bias and loss aversion. Dual-process theory proposes two systems of thought: a fast, automatic (34)........... and a slower, more deliberate one used for complex reasoning. The author argues that many biases likely served as useful (35)........... in ancestral environments. This understanding has influenced (36)........... theory, which subtly adjusts choice architecture to encourage better decisions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"34. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (34). Summary: Traditional economic models assumed that people make decisions through careful (32)........... analysis. However, research has shown those decisions often deviate from this ideal due to (33)........... such as confirmation bias and loss aversion. Dual-process theory proposes two systems of thought: a fast, automatic (34)........... and a slower, more deliberate one used for complex reasoning. The author argues that many biases likely served as useful (35)........... in ancestral environments. This understanding has influenced (36)........... theory, which subtly adjusts choice architecture to encourage better decisions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"35. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (35). Summary: Traditional economic models assumed that people make decisions through careful (32)........... analysis. However, research has shown those decisions often deviate from this ideal due to (33)........... such as confirmation bias and loss aversion. Dual-process theory proposes two systems of thought: a fast, automatic (34)........... and a slower, more deliberate one used for complex reasoning. The author argues that many biases likely served as useful (35)........... in ancestral environments. This understanding has influenced (36)........... theory, which subtly adjusts choice architecture to encourage better decisions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"36. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (36). Summary: Traditional economic models assumed that people make decisions through careful (32)........... analysis. However, research has shown those decisions often deviate from this ideal due to (33)........... such as confirmation bias and loss aversion. Dual-process theory proposes two systems of thought: a fast, automatic (34)........... and a slower, more deliberate one used for complex reasoning. The author argues that many biases likely served as useful (35)........... in ancestral environments. This understanding has influenced (36)........... theory, which subtly adjusts choice architecture to encourage better decisions."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 37–40"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"37. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author believes that cognitive biases prove human decision-making is fundamentally broken."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"38. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author suggests that many biases may have been advantageous in the environments in which humans evolved."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"39. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author has personally designed a nudge-based policy programme for a government."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author argues that nudge theory is entirely free from ethical controversy."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]}]}'::jsonb, 'published', 'advanced', 60, now())
  returning id into v_lesson;

  values (v_lesson, 'Practice: Test 4: Bird Migration / Urbanisation & Health / Decision-Making', 'Grade your answers and see the explanation for every question.', 'advanced', 60, 40, true)
  returning id into v_qset;




































  -- Test 5
  values (v_practice_tests, 'Test 5: The Printing Press / Water Scarcity / Space Tourism', 'test-5', 'A full IELTS Reading practice test (3 passages, 40 questions) from "IELTS Reading Band 5.5 to 8".', '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"callout","attrs":{"variant":"note"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set a timer for 60 minutes (20 minutes per passage) before you start. Attempt the whole test without checking answers, then click Start Practice below to grade yourself and see the explanation for every question."}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 1: The Invention of the Printing Press"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before the mid-fifteenth century, books circulating in Europe were produced almost entirely by hand, typically copied out laboriously by scribes, often working in monasteries. This process was slow and expensive, meaning that books remained rare and costly objects, and literacy was largely confined to the clergy and a small, wealthy elite. In parts of Asia, by contrast, woodblock printing had already been in use for centuries, though the technique had not become widespread in Europe."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Johannes Gutenberg’s crucial innovation, developed in the German city of Mainz in the 1440s, was not printing itself but movable metal type: individual, reusable letter blocks that could be arranged and rearranged to compose different texts. Combined with oil-based ink and a printing press adapted from an existing wine press design, this innovation dramatically increased the speed, and correspondingly reduced the cost, of producing books compared with hand-copying."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Gutenberg’s famous Bible, completed around 1455 and often referred to as the 42-line Bible on account of the number of lines per page, is widely considered the first major book printed in Europe using movable type. Despite the revolutionary nature of his invention, Gutenberg himself did not profit greatly from it: he became embroiled in a financial dispute with his investor, Johann Fust, and ultimately lost control of his own printing workshop and equipment."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The technology spread with striking speed across Europe in the decades that followed. By 1500, printing presses were operating in more than two hundred European cities, having collectively produced an estimated twenty million books — a remarkable figure, given that books had been a genuinely rare commodity only a few decades earlier."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The consequences extended far beyond simple convenience. Wider availability of books contributed directly to rising literacy rates, to a gradual standardisation of language, as printed texts tended to favour certain dialects over regional variation, and to the remarkably rapid spread of new ideas. Historians frequently credit the printing press with playing a crucial role in enabling the spread of Renaissance humanism, the Protestant Reformation — Martin Luther’s writings were disseminated widely through print — and, eventually, the Scientific Revolution."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The printing press is regularly ranked among the most significant inventions in human history for its role in democratising access to information. Some historians draw direct comparisons to the internet’s more recent impact on the spread of information, though most are careful to caution that such comparisons have their limits, given the vastly different technological and social contexts involved."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 1–7"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Before Gutenberg, books in Europe were produced exclusively using woodblock printing."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Gutenberg’s key innovation was the invention of printing itself."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Gutenberg’s printing method combined movable type with a modified wine press."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Gutenberg travelled to Asia to study woodblock printing before developing his invention."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"By 1500, printing presses were operating in more than two hundred European cities."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The printing press has been directly linked by historians to the Protestant Reformation."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"All historians agree that the printing press and the internet have had identical effects on society."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. True"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. False"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 8–13"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. In Europe, books were traditionally produced by hand-copying, often carried out by ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Gutenberg developed his innovation in the German city of ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Gutenberg’s famous Bible, completed around 1455, is often referred to as the ........... Bible."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Gutenberg lost control of his workshop following a dispute with his investor, ........... ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. By 1500, European presses had produced an estimated ........... books."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"13. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the sentences below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Some historians compare the printing press’s impact to that of the ........... ."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2: Global Water Scarcity"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Although the Earth’s surface is mostly covered by water, less than one per cent of the world’s water is readily accessible fresh water suitable for human use; the overwhelming majority is either seawater or locked away in ice caps and glaciers. Billions of people currently experience some form of water scarcity for at least part of the year, and this number is projected to grow further as a result of population growth, climate change, and rising demand from agriculture and industry."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"It is worth distinguishing between two different types of scarcity. Physical scarcity occurs when a region simply does not have enough water resources to meet demand, as is the case in many arid regions. Economic scarcity, by contrast, occurs when adequate water resources exist, but a lack of infrastructure or investment prevents people from actually accessing them. Much of sub-Saharan Africa, despite reasonably adequate rainfall in parts of the region, is frequently cited as an example of economic rather than physical scarcity."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Agriculture accounts for roughly seventy per cent of global freshwater withdrawal, by far the largest single use of fresh water worldwide. Inefficient irrigation techniques in many regions waste a substantial proportion of this water before it ever reaches crops. The adoption of more efficient methods, such as drip irrigation and precision agriculture, can dramatically reduce agricultural water consumption without necessarily reducing crop yields."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Climate change is compounding the problem considerably. It is altering precipitation patterns, causing more frequent and severe droughts in some regions while, somewhat paradoxically, increasing flooding risk in others. Glaciers and mountain snowpack, which have traditionally provided a steady water supply to major rivers through gradual seasonal melt, are shrinking in many parts of the world, threatening the long-term reliability of water supply for populations living downstream."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Many regions rely heavily on groundwater stored in underground aquifers, which in numerous cases are being extracted considerably faster than they can naturally replenish. This is particularly concerning because, unlike surface water, aquifer depletion can be effectively irreversible, or may take many centuries to recover, since most aquifers were filled gradually over geological timescales. Several major agricultural regions around the world are now facing serious long-term aquifer depletion."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"A range of responses is being pursued, including investment in water-efficient technology, wastewater recycling and treatment for reuse, and desalination technology, which, though energy-intensive and historically expensive, is becoming increasingly viable as costs continue to fall. Improved water pricing structures that better reflect true scarcity value, alongside cross-border cooperation agreements covering shared river basins and aquifers, are also being pursued. Experts consistently stress that no single solution alone will be sufficient; a combination of technological, policy and behavioural change will be required."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Question 14-19"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 2 has six paragraphs, A–F. Choose the correct heading for each paragraph from the list of headings below. (Questions 14–19)"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"i. Two different types of scarcity, one problem"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"ii. Why farming consumes so much of the world’s water"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iii. A shrinking source of water is at risk from a warming planet"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"iv. An invisible resource being used faster than it can recover"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"v. A combination of approaches will be needed"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"vi. Why most of Earth’s water cannot be used by humans"}]}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph A"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph B"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph C"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph D"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph E"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paragraph F"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 20–26"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. an example of a region facing scarcity despite adequate rainfall"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"21. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a technique that can reduce water use without lowering crop yields"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"22. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a reference to a resource that may take centuries to recover once depleted"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"23. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Which paragraph contains the following information? Write the correct letter, A–F. a statement that no single measure alone will resolve the problem"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"24. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What percentage of global freshwater withdrawal is used by agriculture?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"25. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What term describes underground water sources being extracted faster than they refill?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"26. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What technology, though energy-intensive, is becoming more viable as costs fall?"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading Passage 3: Space Tourism: Opportunities and Challenges"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Once the exclusive domain of government-employed astronauts, space travel has begun opening up to paying private citizens over the past two decades, driven largely by the emergence of private spaceflight companies developing reusable rocket technology, which has substantially reduced launch costs compared with traditional government space programmes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Current forms of space tourism vary considerably. Suborbital flights, which offer a few brief minutes of weightlessness and a view of the Earth’s curvature without reaching full orbital velocity, are offered by some companies at a relatively lower cost, though still expensive by any ordinary standard. Orbital flights, which involve days spent aboard a spacecraft actually orbiting the Earth, are offered by other providers at dramatically higher price points, and have historically been accessible only to extremely wealthy individuals."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Proponents argue that the space tourism industry could eventually help fund broader space exploration and technology development, and that an expanding pool of people who have personally experienced spaceflight may increase public support and investment in space programmes more generally. Some astronauts report a profound shift in perspective after seeing the Earth from space, sometimes referred to as the “overview effect”."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Critics, meanwhile, raise several concerns. These include the environmental impact of rocket launches, whose carbon emissions and broader atmospheric effects are still being studied and are not yet fully understood; the inherent safety risks of spaceflight, even with modern technology; and a broader question of resource allocation, since critics argue that the vast sums spent enabling wealthy individuals to enjoy brief recreational spaceflight experiences could arguably be directed towards more pressing problems here on Earth."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In my view, rather than treating this as a simple binary question of whether space tourism is good or bad, it is more productive to ask how the industry can be responsibly regulated — through environmental standards for launch providers and transparent safety regulations, for instance — given that the industry appears highly likely to continue growing regardless of any individual’s judgement about its underlying merit. Responsible regulation, rather than outright prohibition, which seems genuinely impractical at this stage, is probably the more realistic and constructive path forward."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 27–31"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"27. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, what has substantially reduced the cost of launching rockets in recent years?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Government subsidies to private companies"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. Reusable rocket technology developed by private companies"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. A reduction in the price of rocket fuel"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. International agreements limiting launch costs"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"28. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What distinguishes orbital spaceflights from suborbital ones, according to the passage?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Orbital flights do not require paying customers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. Orbital flights involve days aboard a spacecraft actually orbiting Earth"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Suborbital flights are more expensive than orbital flights"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Suborbital flights last several days"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"29. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What is the “overview effect” mentioned in the passage?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. A technical fault occurring during re-entry"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. A profound perspective shift some astronauts report after seeing Earth from space"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. A financial overview provided to space tourism customers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. A method used to calculate launch costs"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"According to the passage, what concern do critics raise about resource allocation?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Space tourism companies do not pay any taxes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. Funds spent on space tourism could address pressing problems on Earth"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. There is not enough demand for space tourism to be profitable"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. Rocket fuel is becoming increasingly scarce"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"31. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"What is the author’s own suggested approach to the space tourism industry?"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. The industry should be banned outright"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. The industry should be left completely unregulated"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. The industry should be responsibly regulated rather than prohibited"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"D. The industry should be limited exclusively to government astronauts"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 32–36"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"32. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (32). Summary: Space tourism has become possible partly due to (32)........... rocket technology developed by private companies, which has reduced launch costs. Suborbital flights offer brief weightlessness, while (33)........... flights involve days spent actually circling the Earth. Some astronauts report a profound perspective shift known as the (34)........... after viewing Earth from space. Critics raise concerns about environmental impact, safety, and (35)........... , arguing that funds could instead address problems on Earth. The author suggests that responsible (36)........... , rather than an outright ban, is the more realistic path forward."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"33. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (33). Summary: Space tourism has become possible partly due to (32)........... rocket technology developed by private companies, which has reduced launch costs. Suborbital flights offer brief weightlessness, while (33)........... flights involve days spent actually circling the Earth. Some astronauts report a profound perspective shift known as the (34)........... after viewing Earth from space. Critics raise concerns about environmental impact, safety, and (35)........... , arguing that funds could instead address problems on Earth. The author suggests that responsible (36)........... , rather than an outright ban, is the more realistic path forward."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"34. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (34). Summary: Space tourism has become possible partly due to (32)........... rocket technology developed by private companies, which has reduced launch costs. Suborbital flights offer brief weightlessness, while (33)........... flights involve days spent actually circling the Earth. Some astronauts report a profound perspective shift known as the (34)........... after viewing Earth from space. Critics raise concerns about environmental impact, safety, and (35)........... , arguing that funds could instead address problems on Earth. The author suggests that responsible (36)........... , rather than an outright ban, is the more realistic path forward."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"35. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (35). Summary: Space tourism has become possible partly due to (32)........... rocket technology developed by private companies, which has reduced launch costs. Suborbital flights offer brief weightlessness, while (33)........... flights involve days spent actually circling the Earth. Some astronauts report a profound perspective shift known as the (34)........... after viewing Earth from space. Critics raise concerns about environmental impact, safety, and (35)........... , arguing that funds could instead address problems on Earth. The author suggests that responsible (36)........... , rather than an outright ban, is the more realistic path forward."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"36. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"Complete the summary below. Use NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer. Blank (36). Summary: Space tourism has become possible partly due to (32)........... rocket technology developed by private companies, which has reduced launch costs. Suborbital flights offer brief weightlessness, while (33)........... flights involve days spent actually circling the Earth. Some astronauts report a profound perspective shift known as the (34)........... after viewing Earth from space. Critics raise concerns about environmental impact, safety, and (35)........... , arguing that funds could instead address problems on Earth. The author suggests that responsible (36)........... , rather than an outright ban, is the more realistic path forward."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":4},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Questions 37–40"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"37. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author believes space tourism should be banned immediately."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"38. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author suggests the industry is likely to continue growing regardless of individual opinions about its merit."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"39. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author has personally travelled to space as a tourist."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"40. ","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]},{"type":"text","text":"The author argues that the environmental impact of rocket launches is already fully understood by scientists."}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A. Yes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"B. No"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"C. Not Given"}]}]}]}]}'::jsonb, 'published', 'advanced', 60, now())
